"Byron A Jeff" <byron@cc.gatech.edu> wrote:

[Lots of good reasons to want "weightless AC"
early.]

> I think that diving for Minetown is ill advised.

Agreed. There's only ever two reasons to abandon a
slow and steady approach to Mines Town: you're
starving, or your pet is killed/lost/levelported and
you're surrounded.

> But if you take the time to armor up, gather your
> money/stuff from the Dlvl 2-5 shops, buff the pet,
> and bump up your HP, then make protection your
> first target at a low XL is a reasonable goal.

I'd put that in a different light. Stay on DLVL 1-4
until your pet is HD 9 HP 72(72), plenty easy to do
if you follow or herd your pet instead of expecting
your pet to follow you, stand near your pet while
your pet melees edible monsters, and snatch away
enough corpses from your pet to survive between food
prayers.

This time can also be usefully spent having your pet
test pretty much every item found to be uncursed or
not, even if you have no immediate need for the
items, in bankrupting any shops found, in completely
exploring each level.

Almost always, it is possible to explore the first
four main dungeon levels without accumulating a
single XP. I've gotten into Sokoban with 1 XP a few
times, but that's an exercise in futility, I'm as
likely as not to exit Sokoban at XL 7 anyway.

The trick instead is to turn around and do mines,
dawdling there to try on each and every uncursed
piece of armor dropped there. It is fairly easy to
make it to Mines Town with AC < -5 and XP < 10, if
one plays entirely in sissy mode, even without use
of the E-word. Wands of sleep _really_ help out in
this effort, since they neutralize monsters for your
pet to kill without giving you XP. Wands of slow
monster for the enemy, and speed monster for you and
your pets, are also very useful.

Now drop the 4000 or more zorkmids available from
bankrupting just the Mines Town shops, and you've
got an easy 10 points of weight-free AC. Compared
to five, or three, at the next two higher XLs, this
is much superior, and well worth the effort to
arrive at XL 1.

As for the protection racket being "high risk";
well, maybe. I play almost every game with this
goal, so I'm a pretty experienced protection
racketeer, and the games where I'm diverted into a
different strategy have no better survival rates.
Until you _need_ XL 14, minimizing XL seems like a
winning strategy for as much of the game as I see
these days.

I think the people who find the protection racket
"high risk" just can't be bothered to spend the
time to get good at it.

